<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 1.1, Essential 1.1 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-sets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Sets</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Every day you sort things into groups: the learners in your stream, the subjects you take, the counties that border Lake Victoria. In mathematics a well-defined group like this is called a set, and you have used sets informally since your early grades.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will write sets and their elements in set notation, represent sets in different ways and recognise different types of sets. You will then combine sets using operations such as union and intersection, draw Venn diagrams to show how sets overlap, and apply sets to real-life situations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sets help you organise information and count without counting twice. They are used whenever survey results are sorted, for example when a school records how many learners play football, volleyball or both.
    </p>
  </introduction>

